  "id": "19-liver-clonotype-networks",
  "metadata": {
    "eval_type": "scientific",
    "kit": "liver",
    "task": "clonotype-networks"
  },
  "task": "There's liver transplantation data. There are four types of statuses (Pre-TXP - before transplantation, No-ACR - no rejection, Late-ACR - rejection, resolved - rejection treated successfully). Biopsies were obtained from the same patients several times over time (e.g. BX-1, BX-2, BX-3). For each sample, RNA-seq, TCR-seq and BCR-seq information is available. Load the data, perform preprocessing and quality control. Use a 10% threshold for mitochondrial genes. Filter the RNA-seq, TCR and BCR data independently; do not remove TCR or BCR cells based on RNA-seq quality control. Keep only TCR cells with exactly one productive alpha and one productive beta chain, and BCR cells with exactly one productive heavy and one productive light chain. For the downstream analysis, use TCR data. Define clonotypes by nucleotide sequence identity of both chains across all samples, and call a clonotype expanded if it has more than 2 cells in the whole dataset. Look at patients with multiple biopsies. For patient 11, what fraction of the expanded clonotypes present in their first biopsy are also present in their last biopsy? For patient 12, compute the fraction of cells in expanded clonotypes for each biopsy, and compare consecutive biopsies. Is the largest change between consecutive biopsies one where the clinical status changes (\"between\") or one within the same status (\"inside\")? Output the size of that largest change (absolute difference). Group clonotypes into clusters by CDR3 amino-acid similarity (TCRdist of at most 15 on both chains). Among clusters that contain at least 2 distinct clonotypes and cells from more than one clinical status, how many cells does the largest one have (output -1 if there is none)?"
}
